## <h1 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # My Favorite Athletes! – Lesson # 3</h1>
<p style="text-align: center;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> Famous Panamanians</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> My Favorite Athletes!</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Propose collaboratively the steps to develop a simple project. Interactive: Negotiate the organization of a project or presentation.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can discuss and propose steps to create a project about famous people (e.g., "We can make a poster about Hillary Heron.")
Can negotiate the organization of a simple project (e.g., "Let's write down different tasks for the poster project, and then we can choose the ones we want to do.")
Can describe basic resources for a group project (e.g., "You can use an online biography tool to find more details about Erika Ender's music career." "You can use a dictionary app to find definitions.").</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays pictures of three famous Panamanian athletes (e.g., Roberto Durán, Atheyna Bylon, Hillary Heron) on the board or screen. Students are asked to verbally identify them and share one thing they know about each. Example questions: "Who is this famous athlete?" "What sport does he/she compete in?" "What is one great achievement you know about them?" This activates prior knowledge and vocabulary related to "My Favorite Athletes!".</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Modeling:</b> The teacher models responses using target grammar and vocabulary. Example: "This is Roberto Durán. He is a famous boxer. He won many awards in boxing competitions. He is considered one of the greatest boxers in the world." The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of '-ed' endings in 'won' (irregular but emphasized as an achievement) and 'trained', and '-ing' in 'training'. The teacher also models simple phrases for proposing ideas, like "We can discuss..." or "How about...".</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Clarification (CCQs):</b>
            <ul>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 1: "If someone 'achieved' something, did they succeed or fail?" (Expected: "They succeeded.")</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 2: "If I say 'She is training for a new tournament,' is she doing it now or in the future?" (Expected: "In the future.")</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Material:</b> A short audio recording or a printed dialogue between two 7th-grade students, Carlos and Ana, discussing how to plan a project about "My Favorite Athletes!" for their class. The dialogue explicitly uses present continuous for future, comparatives/superlatives, past simple for achievements, and phrases for proposing steps and negotiating organization.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Example Dialogue Snippet:</b>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Carlos:</b> "Hey Ana, we are planning our 'Famous Panamanians' project, right? Who are you choosing?"</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Ana:</b> "I'm thinking about Erika Ender. She is a talented singer and entertainer. She won many international awards for her music."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Carlos:</b> "Great choice! She's very popular. I'm going with Mariano Rivera. He's arguably the best baseball player. He achieved so much more recognition than other pitchers."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Ana:</b> "Definitely! So, how about we make a presentation? We can discuss their achievements."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Carlos:</b> "That sounds good! We are presenting next week. I can research Mariano's awards, and you can research Erika's early performances. We can use a dictionary app for any new vocabulary."</p>
          </li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Communicative Competence:</b> Interacts orally in different communicative situations.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Comprehension tasks (oral):</b>
            <ul>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What kind of project are Carlos and Ana discussing?"</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Who is Ana's chosen famous Panamanian? What did she achieve?"</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Who is Carlos's chosen famous Panamanian? What makes him stand out?"</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What resources are they planning to use for their project?"</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice speaking using:
            <ul>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Present continuous for future events, Comparatives and superlatives, Past simple for achievements.</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> athlete, achievement, award, singer, recognition, competition, performance, sports fan, win, achieve, inspire, train, perform, compete, famous, popular, talented, skilled, more, best.</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Practice pronunciation of '-ing' verb endings (e.g., "watching," "training") and '-ed' in past tense (e.g., "performed," "achieved").</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Describing achievements ("Mariano Rivera won many awards."), Comparing figures ("Sech is more popular than other singers.").</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Detailed Procedure:</b>
            <ul>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Oral Practice: Sentence Builders.</b> The teacher provides sentence frames or cues on the board for students to complete orally. Students take turns constructing sentences individually, focusing on correct grammar and pronunciation.
                <ul>
                  <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 1 (Past Simple): "Atheyna Bylon ______ (win) a medal in the Olympic Games. She ______ (achieve) great recognition." (Student: "Atheyna Bylon *won* a medal in the Olympic Games. She *achieved* great recognition.")</li>
                  <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 2 (Comparatives/Superlatives): "Roberto Durán is ______ (famous) boxer than other boxers. He is one of the ______ (good) in the world." (Student: "Roberto Durán is *a more famous* boxer than other boxers. He is one of the *best* in the world.")</li>
                  <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 3 (Present Continuous for Future): "Hillary Heron ______ (train) for a new competition. She ______ (perform) a new routine next month." (Student: "Hillary Heron *is training* for a new competition. She *is performing* a new routine next month.")</li>
                </ul>
              </li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-controlled Practice: "Compare and Describe."</b> In pairs, students receive cards with names of two Panamanian famous figures (e.g., "Roberto Durán & Mariano Rivera" or "Erika Ender & Sech"). They must orally compare and describe their achievements and popularity using comparatives, superlatives, past simple, and present continuous. Teacher circulates, providing feedback on accuracy and pronunciation, especially '-ed' and '-ing' sounds.</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Pair Interaction: "Project Planning Chat."</b> The teacher instructs students to think about their own favorite Panamanian athlete. In pairs, students orally propose ideas for a simple project about their chosen athlete (e.g., a poster, a short presentation). They must verbally negotiate basic steps and potential resources. Example phrases encouraged: "We can make a poster about [athlete's name].", "Let's find pictures.", "I think we should include their achievements.", "You can research their awards, and I can describe their training.", "What resources can we use? Maybe an online biography tool or a dictionary app?"</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Communicative Task: "Our Famous Athlete Project Proposal."</b></li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Task Format:</b> Collaborative paired oral presentation of a project plan.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Procedure:</b>
            <ul>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. Students continue working in their pairs from Stage 3. They collaboratively choose one famous Panamanian athlete they both admire from the "My Favorite Athletes!" theme.</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Their task is to verbally propose the steps for a simple project (e.g., a poster, a short oral presentation, a fact sheet) about their chosen athlete. They must negotiate the organization of the project, clearly outlining who does what and what resources they will use.</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. Each pair prepares a short (2-3 minute) oral presentation of their project proposal for the class. They should include:
                <ul>
                  <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">- The chosen athlete and why they are famous (using adjectives like 'talented', 'skilled', 'popular', comparatives/superlatives).</li>
                  <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">- Key achievements (using past simple, e.g., "They won...", "They achieved...").</li>
                  <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">- The proposed project format (e.g., "We are making a poster").</li>
                  <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">- The steps they will take (e.g., "First, we are finding pictures.", "Then, I am writing about their awards, and my partner is describing their training."). (Using present continuous for future events).</li>
                  <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">- Resources they might use (e.g., "You can use an online biography tool to find more details," "We can use a dictionary app.").</li>
                </ul>
              </li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. Pairs present their proposals to the class. The teacher facilitates by encouraging questions and comments from other students.</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Focus:</b> Fluency, collaborative interaction, successful negotiation, and comprehensive use of target grammar, vocabulary, and pronunciation in proposing a project.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy and student self-reflection)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist. The teacher uses a simple checklist to note students' use of target language during the "Project Proposal" activity, focusing on their ability to propose collaboratively and negotiate organization.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Assessment Task:</b> The students' paired "Our Famous Athlete Project Proposal" presentation.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Evaluation Criteria:</b>
            <ul>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear articulation of words, including correct pronunciation of '-ing' and '-ed' endings.</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> (e.g., athlete, achievement, award, win, talented, famous, more, best, train, compete, perform).</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target grammar:</b> (e.g., past simple for achievements, present continuous for future plans, comparatives/superlatives).</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and organization:</b> The proposed project steps are easy to understand.</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration and negotiation:</b> Both partners actively contribute to proposing steps and negotiating tasks.</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task Completion:</b> The pair successfully describes a project plan with steps and resources.</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket (Verbal):</b> Students respond to the following prompts before leaving class:
            <ul>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. "One new phrase I learned to use when talking about project ideas is..."</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. "I feel (easy/difficult) using comparatives to talk about athletes because..."</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-8 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Student and teacher reflection, connection to next lesson)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Student self-reflection prompt (oral or written in notebook):</b>
            <ul>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. "What part of proposing your project was the most interesting for you?"</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. "How did practicing with your partner help you speak more clearly about the project steps?"</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Teacher reflection:</b>
            <ul>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson to promote speaking fluency and accuracy, especially with comparatives/superlatives and project negotiation? (e.g., The chain dialogue activity seemed effective for controlled practice, students enjoyed discussing their favorite athletes).</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students to confidently propose and negotiate more complex project ideas in the future? (e.g., Perhaps more explicit scaffolding for using present continuous for future plans, or providing a wider range of resources for students to suggest).</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> "Great job speaking and proposing your project ideas today! In our next lesson, Lesson 4 (Writing Composition), we will use these verbal project proposals and turn them into a short written report or paragraph about our chosen famous Panamanian athlete, focusing on sentence structure and descriptive language."</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>